@node	0.6, 0.7, 0.5, Chapter 0
@section Errors as values

This section covers the edition's error type and how this book uses it. No
exception crosses library code in this book: a fallible operation returns
its error as an ordinary value, and the caller decides where it becomes a
result.

@heading @code{Result}: the two-sided value

The book's two-sided result is one discriminated union, @code{Result}:

@example typescript
type Result<A, E> =
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Ok"; readonly value: A @}
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Error"; readonly error: E @};

const ok = <A>(value: A): Result<A, never> => (@{ _tag: "Ok", value @});
const err = <E>(error: E): Result<never, E> => (@{ _tag: "Error", error @});
@end example

@noindent
A fallible operation returns @code{Ok} of the value on success and
@code{Error} of the failure otherwise. The failure type is part of the
signature, so a caller can see what can go wrong before calling. The
parse errors of this section's example are one variant per failure mode,
the union of @ref{0.4} again:

@example typescript
type PortError =
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Blank" @}
  | @{ readonly _tag: "NotDigits"; readonly input: string @}
  | @{ readonly _tag: "OutOfRange"; readonly n: number @};

const parsePort = (s: string): Result<number, PortError> => @{
  const t = s.trim();
  if (t.length === 0) @{
    return err(@{ _tag: "Blank" @});
  @}
  if (!/^[0-9]+$/.test(t)) @{
    return err(@{ _tag: "NotDigits", input: s @});
  @}
  const n = Number(t);
  if (n < 1 || n > 65535) @{
    return err(@{ _tag: "OutOfRange", n @});
  @}
  return ok(n);
@};
@end example

@noindent
Since no error throws, the code after a guard needs no branch for the
failure: the guard returns, and the rest of the body is the success path.

@example typescript
parsePort(" 8080 ")
// => @{ _tag: "Ok", value: 8080 @}

parsePort("80x0")
// => @{ _tag: "Error", error: @{ _tag: "NotDigits", input: "80x0" @} @}

parsePort("99999")
// => @{ _tag: "Error", error: @{ _tag: "OutOfRange", n: 99999 @} @}
@end example

@heading Per-domain error sets

The error type is a union of the domain, one variant per failure mode, so
a caller can dispatch on errors with the exhaustive @code{switch} of
@ref{0.4}:

@example typescript
const renderError = (e: PortError): string => @{
  switch (e._tag) @{
    case "Blank":
      return "blank input";
    case "NotDigits":
      return `not digits: $@{e.input@}`;
    case "OutOfRange":
      return `out of range: $@{e.n@}`;
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
This book keeps error sets per domain: the account of @ref{3.1} carries
@code{AccountError}, the interval arithmetic of @ref{2.1.4} carries
@code{IntervalError}, and so on. From @ref{3.1} the failures themselves
move onto Effect's typed error channel, and the classes there are
@code{Schema.TaggedError} classes; chapters 1 and 2 keep plain
@code{Result} values like this one and import no Effect module at all.
One error set is shared by every chapter from 4 on that runs interpreted
programs: the evaluator's error classes, whose variants carry the book's
runtime faults, from an unbound name to a wrong arity.

@heading What this buys

Three consequences follow and are used without further comment later.
Errors compose: a helper that returns @code{Result} composes with any
other, and no try-and-rethrow plumbing appears. Errors stay typed all the
way to the driver: an error value is data that tests can compare, which
is how the exercises of @ref{3.1} pin failure behavior. And test failure
paths cost the same as success paths: a probe asserts
@code{err(@{ _tag: "Blank" @})} where an exception-based design would
assert a throw. The runnable version of this section's listings is
@file{packages/ch0/src/06-errors.ts}, asserted by the test file beside
it.

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.5}Exercise 0.5:} The scaffold gives you
@code{parseAmountEager}, a parser for non-negative amounts that throws
@code{RangeError} on blank input and @code{TypeError} on input containing
any character outside @code{0..9} after trimming spaces, alongside a
@code{ParseError} union with @code{Blank} and @code{NotDigits} variants.
Write @code{parseAmount} with the same behavior, returning
@code{Result<number, ParseError>} instead of throwing. Test both sides:
digits with surrounding spaces parse to their value, blank input is a
@code{Blank} error, a stray character is a @code{NotDigits} error
carrying the original input, and the eager original still throws where
your version returns a value.

